#!/usr/bin/env perl
# PODNAME: shotgun
# ABSTRACT: mass upload of files via SCP/FTP/...

binmode STDOUT, ":utf8";

use App::Shotgun;
App::Shotgun->new_with_options->shot;

__END__

=pod

=head1 SYNOPSIS

  shotgun --filelist a_filelist.txt \
	--target FTP:hostname=localhost:username=testftp:password=test
  shotgun --source /path/to/site --file first_file.txt --file second_file.txt \
	--target FTP:hostname=localhost:username=testftp:password=test \
	--target SFTP:hostname=remote.server.com:username=testftp:path=/srv/www

=head1 DESCRIPTION

C<shotgun> sends every file of a file list to every target, on every run. It
compares nothing - no size, no timestamp, no checksum - and that is the point.

The file list comes from C<--filelist>, from one or more C<--file> options or,
with C<--filerecursive>, from the C<--source> directory. The targets are given
as C<--target> options. Each file is uploaded to the first target, then to the
second one and so on; only when all targets have the file the next file
follows. With C<--parallel> all targets receive the same file at the same
time. Missing directories on a target are created one level at a time.

The first error stops the run: all targets are shut down and C<shotgun> exits
with an exit code above 0.

=head1 OPTIONS

=head2 --filelist

Path to a text file listing the files to upload, one per line, relative to
C<--source>. An alternative to C<--file>.

=head2 --file

A file to upload, relative to C<--source>. Can be given several times. An
alternative to C<--filelist>; if both are given, the C<--file> options win.

=head2 --target

A target as C<type:key=value:key=value...>. The type is C<FTP> or C<SFTP>; the
keys are the attributes of the transport, e.g. C<hostname>, C<username>,
C<password>, C<path> and C<port>. Can be given several times. A target without
a C<username> takes its login from F<~/.netrc>.

Example: C<FTP:hostname=ftp.example.com:username=user:password=pass:path=/htdocs>

A colon cannot be part of a value.

=head2 --source

The local directory the files are relative to. The default is the current
working directory.

=head2 --filerecursive

Take the file list from the C<--source> directory, recursively, instead of
from C<--filelist> or C<--file>.

=head2 --parallel

Upload each file to all targets at the same time instead of one target after
the other.

=head2 --transferlog

Append one line per successful transfer to this file.

=cut
